<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ashvaghosha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 80–c. 150 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> · Also known as Aśvaghoṣa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ashvaghosha was an Indian Buddhist philosopher, dramatist and poet, regarded as one of the first great masters of classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> poetry. His Buddhacarita, an epic life of the Buddha, and the Saundarananda are the best-known works attributed to him. Later Buddhist tradition places him at the court of the Kushan emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanishka">Kanishka</a>, although the association cannot be confirmed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashvaghosha.jpg" alt="Ashvaghosha" width="185" height="280" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ashvaghosha.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
